 ## Overview
-Opacity Split for Cloning is a method to initialize the opacity of cloned Gaussians to maintain transmittance consistency, preventing the cloned child from having less impact than the parent alone [S2]. It solves the problem where standard cloning inherits the parent's full opacity, which artificially increases the total transmittance attenuation of the parent-child pair compared to the original single Gaussian, thereby biasing the alpha blending process [S2]. It sets itself apart from generic cloning by explicitly deriving new opacity values from the parent's opacity to preserve the cumulative transmittance $t_k$ before and after the split [S2].
+Opacity Split for Cloning is a method used during the densification phase of 3D Gaussian Splatting to clone existing Gaussians while preserving the visual fidelity of the rendered image. It solves the problem of "transmittance bias" introduced when a Gaussian is duplicated: in standard alpha blending, a cloned child inheriting the parent's full opacity causes the combined transmittance to drop more than intended, artificially darkening the background or subsequent primitives [S2]. The method ensures that the rasterization outcome remains unchanged by adjusting the opacity of the parent and clones according to a specific splitting formula [S1, S2].
 
 ## How it works
